      "id": 1,
      "text": "Millville earned its name from the sorghum, lumber, and grist mills that once dotted the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Millville%2C_Delaware",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s founders were families of ship captains, watermen, millers, and farmers who had lived there for centuries.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://millville.delaware.gov/community/history/",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "When settlers first arrived, marshes covered much of the area, so plantations were built on higher ground near present‑day Millville.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://millville.delaware.gov/community/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1737, King George II forbade new settlements in Delaware’s lower counties until further royal approval.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
